  "explainWhatDoesThisMean": "What does this mean?",
  "explainHowToRead": "How to read this",
  "explainLimitations": "Limitations & Guidance",
  "explainDisclaimer": "Vedic astrology provides guidance and self-reflection; it does not replace medical, legal, or financial professional advice.",
  "explainBirthChartTitle": "Understanding Your Kundli",
  "explainBirthChartWhatIs": "Your Kundli is a snapshot of the celestial sky at the exact moment and location of your birth, mapped across 12 zodiac houses (Bhavas) and signs (Rashis).",
  "explainBirthChartHowToRead": "House 1 (top central diamond) represents your Ascendant (Lagna) and core personality. The numbers 1–12 indicate zodiac signs, and abbreviations like 'Su' or 'Mo' show planetary placements.",
  "explainPlanetsTitle": "Understanding Planetary Placements",
  "explainPlanetsWhatIs": "In Vedic astrology, each of the nine grahas (Sun to Ketu) governs specific life themes and drives distinct psychological energies.",
  "explainPlanetsHowToRead": "Look at the sign and house position for each planet. A planet marked '(R)' is retrograde, meaning its energy is reflective, unconventional, and karmically potent.",
  "explainDashaTitle": "Understanding Vimshottari Dasha",
  "explainDashaWhatIs": "Vimshottari Dasha is a 120-year planetary timeline that reveals which celestial energy currently influences your life events and consciousness.",
  "explainDashaHowToRead": "The active Mahadasha (major period) sets the broader theme, while the Antardasha (sub-period) highlights daily focus. Check the start and end dates to understand active cycles.",
  "explainHousesTitle": "Understanding the 12 Houses (Bhavas)",
  "explainHousesWhatIs": "In Vedic astrology, the 12 houses divide your life into distinct spheres of experience, from self, wealth, and family to career, partnerships, and spiritual liberation.",
  "explainHousesHowToRead": "House 1 (Tanu Bhava) represents your physical self and character. Houses with occupying planets emphasize those themes in your life, while unoccupied houses are guided by their ruling sign lord.",
  "explainNakshatraTitle": "Understanding Nakshatras (Lunar Mansions)",
  "explainNakshatraWhatIs": "Nakshatras are 27 lunar constellations that provide deeper psychological insight into your temperament, natural talents, and Vimshottari timing cycles.",
  "explainNakshatraHowToRead": "Your Janma Nakshatra is where the Moon was positioned at birth. Its ruling planet sets your starting life dasha, and the Pada (quarter 1–4) indicates subtle inner strengths.",
  "explainPanchangaTitle": "Understanding Daily Panchanga",
  "explainPanchangaWhatIs": "Panchanga represents the five limbs of time: Tithi (lunar phase), Vara (weekday), Nakshatra (constellation), Yoga (luni-solar angle), and Karana (half-tithi).",
  "explainPanchangaHowToRead": "Check Tithi and Nakshatra for general auspiciousness, and observe Rahu Kaal to avoid starting critical new ventures during that window.",
  "explainMatchmakingTitle": "Understanding Kundli Matchmaking",
  "explainMatchmakingWhatIs": "Ashtakoota Milan evaluates compatibility across eight psychological and physiological dimensions, totaling a maximum of 36 points (Gunas).",
  "explainMatchmakingHowToRead": "A score of 18 or higher is traditionally considered acceptable. Higher scores indicate greater natural alignment in temperament, emotional bonding (Bhakoot), and genetic vitality (Nadi).",
  "explainShadbalaTitle": "Understanding Shadbala Strengths",
  "explainShadbalaWhatIs": "Shadbala evaluates six distinct sources of astronomical strength: Sthana (positional), Dig (directional), Kala (temporal), Cheshta (motional), Naisargika (natural), and Drik (aspectual).",
  "explainShadbalaHowToRead": "Each planet has a minimum required strength measured in Virupas and Rupas. Planets with a ratio of 1.0 or greater possess sufficient strength to manifest their positive significations.",
  "explainAshtakavargaTitle": "Understanding Ashtakavarga",
  "explainAshtakavargaWhatIs": "Ashtakavarga maps the distribution of benefic points (bindus) contributed by eight references across the 12 signs of the zodiac.",
  "explainAshtakavargaHowToRead": "In Sarvashtakavarga (SAV), houses with 28 or more bindus represent strong, supportive life areas, while houses below 25 require patience and mindful effort.",
  "explainDivisionalChartsTitle": "Understanding Divisional Charts (Vargas)",
  "explainDivisionalChartsWhatIs": "Divisional charts (Shodashvarga) divide the 30° zodiac signs into microscopic harmonic segments to examine specialized life domains like marriage (D9), career (D10), and destiny (D60).",
  "explainDivisionalChartsHowToRead": "D1 (Rashi) shows the physical manifestation, while higher vargas reveal specific karmic potentials. A strong planet in D1 that is also exalted or in its own sign in D9 (Vargottama) brings enduring favorable outcomes.",
  "explainBhavaBalaTitle": "Understanding Bhava Bala (House Potency)",
  "explainBhavaBalaWhatIs": "Bhava Bala evaluates the comprehensive strength of each of the 12 astrological houses based on Lord strength, directional cusp potency, aspects, and resident planets.",
  "explainBhavaBalaHowToRead": "Houses with higher Rupas (typically 6.0+ or ranked 1–4) indicate areas of life that flourish with ease and resilience, while lower-ranked houses need conscious nurturing.",
  "explainYogasTitle": "Understanding Vedic Yogas",
  "explainYogasWhatIs": "Yogas are special planetary combinations described in classical scriptures (Brihat Parashara Hora Shastra) that generate extraordinary strengths, wealth, leadership, or spiritual wisdom.",
  "explainYogasHowToRead": "Active yogas are formed when planetary relationships meet exact scriptural conditions. Check the involved planets, house alignments, and condition checklist to understand how the yoga functions.",
  "explainJaiminiTitle": "Understanding Jaimini Astrology",
  "explainJaiminiWhatIs": "Jaimini Sutras use seven or eight Chara Karakas (variable planetary significators determined by degrees) and sign-based aspects (Rashi Drishti) to reveal soul purpose and destiny.",
  "explainJaiminiHowToRead": "Atmakaraka (AK) represents your soul's journey, Amatyakaraka (AmK) indicates career and advisors, and Darakaraka (DK) signifies partnerships and spouse. Arudha Lagna (AL) reflects your external societal image.",
  "explainKpTitle": "Understanding KP Astrology (Krishnamurti)",
  "explainKpWhatIs": "Krishnamurti Padhdhati (KP) uses Placidus house cusps and a 4-tier sub-lord system (Sign Lord, Star Lord, Sub Lord, Sub-Sub Lord) to time events with pinpoint precision.",
  "explainKpHowToRead": "In KP, a planet or cusp's primary outcome is determined by its Star Lord, while the final fruitful manifestation is governed by its Sub Lord. Examine the sub-lord connections for accurate event timing.",
  "explainGocharaTitle": "Understanding Gochara (Transits)",
  "explainGocharaWhatIs": "Gochara evaluates current planetary movements in the sidereal sky relative to your natal Moon sign, Ascendant, and Ashtakavarga energy matrix.",
  "explainGocharaHowToRead": "Benefic transits over houses with 28+ SAV bindus unlock opportunities, while challenging transits (like Saturn over 12th, 1st, or 2nd house during Sade Sati) require disciplined focus and caution.",
  "explainSpecialPointsTitle": "Understanding Special Points & Upagrahas",
  "explainSpecialPointsWhatIs": "Vedic astrology calculates subtle non-luminous energy centers including 5 shadow Upagrahas (Dhooma to Upaketu), time-based Gulika/Mandi, and Special Lagnas (Hora, Ghatika, Indu).",
  "explainSpecialPointsHowToRead": "Indu Lagna reveals financial prosperity, Hora Lagna evaluates wealth timing, and 64th Navamsha / 22nd Drekkana highlight sensitive vulnerability degrees requiring spiritual remedies.",
  "explainVarshaphalaTitle": "Understanding Varshaphala (Solar Return)",
  "explainVarshaphalaWhatIs": "Varshaphala (Tajika system) calculates an annual horoscope for the precise moment the transiting Sun returns to its exact natal birth longitude.",
  "explainVarshaphalaHowToRead": "The Year Lord (Varshesha) and the moving focus point (Muntha) set the primary annual theme, while Harsha Bala measures the year's psychological and material contentment.",
